WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:05.890
Thanks to the work of some very fine dramatists,
the Netflix adaptation of the Series of Unfortunate

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:10.920
Events novels has been absolutely fantastic,
a word which here means full of very frightening

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.460
danger, various fakery disguises, and very
fitting definitions.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:19.200
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and today,
I'm exploring 12 of the biggest differences

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.910
between the Unfortunate Events TV series and
the original books.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.720
Spoilers ahead for the show and novels.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:29.680
While the books are rather cryptic about the
identity of the taxi driver who offers to

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.391
take the Baudelaires away from the Hotel Denouement
after Dewey's death by harpoon, the TV show

00:00:34.391 --> 00:00:36.230
is much, much clearer.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:38.940
"Violet, Klaus, and Sunny Baudelaire."

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:44.440
"My name is Lemony Snicket."

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.760
In the novels, the children couldn't see the
taxi driver's face in the darkness, just the

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:50.510
tip of a lit cigarette.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:55.079
Of course, even before season 3, Lemony was
already more involved and present in the action

00:00:55.079 --> 00:00:57.079
of the Netflix series compared to the books.

00:00:57.079 --> 00:00:59.540
"when we hear Uncle Monty tell the children..."

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:03.670
"I promise you, no harm will come to you in
the Reptile Room."

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:07.109
"... we should be on guard for the unpleasant
arrival of dramatic irony."

00:01:07.109 --> 00:01:11.479
But it was great to finally see the Baudelaires
and our narrator meet in the Penultimate Peril

00:01:11.479 --> 00:01:12.850
episodes.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:17.299
The whole cast has been a joy to watch throughout
the series, with Patrick Warburton fantastic

00:01:17.299 --> 00:01:21.659
as Lemony Snicket, perfectly capturing his
deadpan nature.

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:26.219
One character who doesn't make it from the
books into the TV show is Captain Widdershins.

00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:30.969
In Season 3, we just see a prominently placed
portrait of him as well as a photograph belonging

00:01:30.969 --> 00:01:35.170
to his step-daughter Fiona, who reveals that
Widdershins left one day to answer a distress

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:37.679
call from a manatee and never came back.

00:01:37.679 --> 00:01:42.439
The TV series combines parts of Fiona's character
from the Grim Grotto book with aspects of

00:01:42.439 --> 00:01:45.329
her step-father's role as Captain of the Queequeg.

00:01:45.329 --> 00:01:50.189
We do hear Widdershins' voice in the finale,
showing that he is indeed still alive!

00:01:50.189 --> 00:01:55.049
Netflix makes several more changes to the
Grim Grotto storyline, for example, Olaf finds

00:01:55.049 --> 00:01:59.649
the Queequeg earlier in the show than he does
in the books, and he forces the Baudelaires

00:01:59.649 --> 00:02:04.349
to visit the Gorgonian Grotto, whereas in
the books, he turns up after the orphans return

00:02:04.349 --> 00:02:08.319
from the Grotto with Fiona, who doesn't accompany
them there in the TV show.

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:13.500
And in the books, Phil goes missing part way
through the story along with Captain Widdershins.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:18.470
The show also reveals how the Hook-Handed
Man, aka Fernald, lost both his hands, which

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:21.420
isn't confirmed in the novels, though it has
been a fan theory.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:26.280
I suspect these various changes were made
to condense the action, highlight the Baudelaires'

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.700
self-reliance by removing an adult guardian
figure, as well as give fans some closure

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.500
on their theories.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:37.630
Because the show's writers wanted Count Olaf
to end Season 2 and begin Season 3 on a triumphant

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:41.910
note, they decided to keep his villainous
associates alive even though several of them

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:43.750
are killed off earlier in the books.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:48.410
For example, in the novels, the Henchperson
of Indeterminate Gender is last seen at Heimlich

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:53.570
Hospital where they likely died in the fire,
and the Bald Man is eaten alive by lions in

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:55.400
The Carnivorous Carnival book.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:59.810
In the TV show, however, both those henchpeople
are still part of Olaf's troupe throughout

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:01.640
The Slippery Slope episodes.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:06.100
As for the White-Faced Women, on the Netflix
series they abandon Olaf's troupe, after he

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:09.890
tells them to throw Sunny off the mountain,
as he also did in the tenth novel.

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:14.070
However, in a change from the book, the Bald
Man and The Henchperson of Indeterminate Gender

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:18.620
join the White-Faced Women in quitting Olaf's
team, and, as they leave, they discuss their

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:21.890
future plans which involve farming and music
halls.

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:25.690
We last see them on stage together at the
end of a show they've put on, enjoying the

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:27.730
audience's applause.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:32.090
Unlike the members of Olaf's villainous theatre
troupe, the Carnival Freaks last a lot less

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:35.060
time in the TV series than they do in the
books.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:39.020
In the original stories, Colette, Hugo, and
Kevin are still alive and kicking and very

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:43.540
much part of Olaf's wicked crew at the Hotel
Denouement and they even provide evidence

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:47.640
at Olaf and the Baudelaire's trial, though
it's unclear whether they make it out of the

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:48.720
hotel fire alive.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:53.280
However, in the TV adaptation, the former
carnival employees are bumped off in the Mortmain

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.420
Mountains by the Man With A Beard But No Hair
and the Woman With Hair But No Beard, soon

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.380
after the start of Season 3.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:03.780
I imagine this was done to gradually chip
away at Olaf and his schemes, bringing him

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:07.480
down from his literal and figurative highest
point at the start of the Season.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:12.290
And, of course, it also gives us some insight
into the show's two newest villains.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:16.620
During Season 3, we also learn more than we
do in the books about Count Olaf's relationship

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:20.310
to the Man With A Beard But No Hair and the
Woman With Hair But No Beard.

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:24.830
When Olaf first sees the ominous duo in the
TV show, he calls them 'mummy' and 'daddy'.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:29.699
Obviously, we see Olaf's actual father during
the opera flashbacks, so these two terrible

00:04:29.699 --> 00:04:34.900
people are not his real parents, but rather
his figurative parents or mentors, who recruited

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:38.939
him to their nefarious ranks when he was at
a low point in his life, after the death of

00:04:38.939 --> 00:04:39.939
his father.

00:04:39.939 --> 00:04:43.830
By adding some backstory between these two
new characters and Olaf in the show, it gives

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:48.949
us some extra context as to what led him down
this dark, fire-filled path in life.

00:04:48.949 --> 00:04:52.550
And it's interesting to see how this man and
woman are so much more impressed with Esmé,

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:57.229
who they've not met before, than they are
with Olaf, who's so desperate for their approval.

00:04:57.229 --> 00:05:02.230
The TV series leaves the Quagmires and the
Widdershins on a somewhat hopeful note, with

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:07.379
the Quagmires finally reuniting; and Fiona
and Fernald finding their stepfather, Captain

00:05:07.379 --> 00:05:08.379
Widdershins.

00:05:08.379 --> 00:05:11.710
However, the books leave us on a much vaguer
and less optimistic note when it comes to

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:12.749
their fates.

00:05:12.749 --> 00:05:17.090
In the novels, Quigley does reunite with his
siblings and helps them deal with the eagles

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:22.520
sent to attack their self-sustaining hot-air
mobile home, but the birds manage to pop the

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:27.600
balloons, sending everyone crashing into the
Queequeg below, which wrecks the submarine.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.479
The Widdershins and Kit Snicket, who was on
the sub at the time, are all tossed into the

00:05:31.479 --> 00:05:35.569
sea, along with the Quagmires and Hector from
the Village of Fowl Devotees.

00:05:35.569 --> 00:05:40.590
Then the Great Unknown appears, and all of
them, apart from Kit, disappear, "either swallowed

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:43.849
up or rescued by that mysterious thing".

00:05:43.849 --> 00:05:48.600
The way the TV show ends the Quagmires and
Widdershins' stories gives fans a little more

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:53.039
closure, which many have enjoyed, though some
prefer a more unfortunate ending like the

00:05:53.039 --> 00:05:54.249
books.

00:05:54.249 --> 00:05:58.889
In the TV version of events, when a heavily-pregnant
Beatrice and her husband Bertrand decide to

00:05:58.889 --> 00:06:03.210
leave the Island, it's because they choose
to return home in time for the birth of their

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:07.370
first child, Violet, as they realise they
can't shelter their family there forever.

00:06:07.370 --> 00:06:11.370
This is rather different from the books where
the couple are forced to abandon the Island

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:16.050
after Ishmael arrives and begins spreading
fears among the islanders about Beatrice and

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:17.699
Bertrand's leadership.

00:06:17.699 --> 00:06:22.169
Ishmael's background as the founder of VFD
and Principal of Prufrock Prep is also new

00:06:22.169 --> 00:06:27.300
to the series, as in the books, he's simply
a former member of VFD and chemistry teacher.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.779
These changes were likely made because there's
just one episode to wrap up the whole story,

00:06:31.779 --> 00:06:35.999
and the mutiny against the Baudelaire parents
would have needed more build-up to make sense.

00:06:35.999 --> 00:06:40.779
As for Ishmael's new backstory, well, that's
very much in keeping with the TV series which

00:06:40.779 --> 00:06:44.120
has been dropping clues and adding intriguing
storylines about VFD since the beginning.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:45.150
By the way, I explain a lot more about the
TV show's ending, including the Sugar Bowl,

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:46.150
VFD, the Schism, and much more in my Ending
Explained video.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:47.150
You can tap in the top right to watch that
or click the link in the video description.

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:50.900
Instead of bringing the third and final season
to a close with Violet, Klaus, Sunny and baby

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:55.719
Beatrice sailing away from the island, leaving
their fate ambiguous, as happens in Chapter

00:06:55.719 --> 00:07:00.850
14 of the last Unfortunate Events book; the
Netflix series continues past this point,

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:05.419
skipping ahead ten or so years, and shows
Lemony Snicket receiving a letter from his

00:07:05.419 --> 00:07:09.289
niece, Beatrice Baudelaire the Second, and
meeting up with her at a soda bar where she

00:07:09.289 --> 00:07:12.930
tells him what happened to the Baudelaires
after they left the island.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:17.169
By continuing the story like this, the TV
show is taking a page out of another of Lemony

00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:21.620
Snicket's books called The Beatrice Letters,
which, as well as correspondence between Lemony

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:26.160
and the Baudelaires' mother, also contains
letters from Kit's daughter Beatrice, asking

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:34.029
Lemony for help tracking down Violet, Klaus,
and Sunny [as they've become separated at

00:07:34.029 --> 00:07:39.680
some point].

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:45.599
Now, what did you think of the changes in
the TV series?

00:07:45.599 --> 00:07:48.639
And what are your favourite moments in the
show and the books?

